<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Votes for Women</h1><p class="path-tagline">From Wollstonecraft's Vindication to the Swiss vote: the long campaign for women's suffrage.</p><p class="path-intro">The organised campaign for women's votes is usually dated from the Seneca Falls Convention of 1848, and it won its first national victory in New Zealand in 1893. Suffragists used petitions, conventions, lobbying and marches, and in Britain the militant suffragettes smashed windows, set fires and went on hunger strike. Much of Europe and North America gave women the vote around the First World War, while many women of colour and colonial subjects waited decades longer. This path begins with the feminist argument of the 1790s and follows the campaigners and the laws to Switzerland, where women won the federal vote only in 1971.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Thinker</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1759–1797</span><span class="path-step-name">Mary Wollstonecraft</span><span class="path-step-why">In A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) Wollstonecraft argued that women only appeared inferior because they lacked education, and should be treated as rational beings.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Mary Wollstonecraft was an English writer and philosopher, regarded as one of the founding feminist philosophers.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/ideology/feminism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 1792 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Feminism</span><span class="path-step-why">Since the late 18th century feminist movements have campaigned for women's right to vote, hold office, work and own property; the suffrage movements were their first wave.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Feminism is a range of movements and ideologies, originating in late 18th-century Europe, which hold that societies are patriarchal and seek the equality of the sexes.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1848</span><span class="path-step-name">Seneca Falls Convention</span><span class="path-step-why">The first women's rights convention, held in July 1848, adopted a Declaration of Sentiments, and its resolution on suffrage passed after Frederick Douglass spoke for it.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Seneca Falls Convention was the first women's rights convention, held in the Wesleyan Chapel at Seneca Falls, New York, on 19–20 July 1848.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1820–1906</span><span class="path-step-name">Susan B. Anthony</span><span class="path-step-why">Anthony founded the National Woman Suffrage Association with Elizabeth Cady Stanton in 1869, and used her trial for voting in 1872 to argue for women's citizenship.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Susan B. Anthony was an American campaigner for women's rights and the abolition of slavery, and the leading organiser of the movement for women's suffrage.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/kate-sheppard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kate-sheppard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1848–1934</span><span class="path-step-name">Kate Sheppard</span><span class="path-step-why">Sheppard organised three petitions between 1891 and 1893, the last signed by 31,872 women, before New Zealand became the first self-governing country where women could vote.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Kate Sheppard was a New Zealand suffragist who led the campaign for women's right to vote.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/womens-social-and-political-union.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1848 CE – 1971 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Women's suffrage movement</span><span class="path-step-why">From 1848 to 1971 suffragists used petitions, conventions, lobbying and marches, and won women the vote and the right to stand for election in most of the world's states.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/emmeline-pankhurst"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emmeline-pankhurst.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1858–1928</span><span class="path-step-name">Emmeline Pankhurst</span><span class="path-step-why">Pankhurst founded the Women's Social and Political Union in 1903, whose suffragettes turned from processions to window-smashing and arson; she was imprisoned repeatedly.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Emmeline Pankhurst was a British political activist who led the militant campaign for women's suffrage.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/representation-of-the-people-act-1918"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/representation-of-the-people-act-1918.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1918</span><span class="path-step-name">Representation of the People Act 1918</span><span class="path-step-why">Passed at the end of the First World War, the Act gave most women over 30 the vote for the first time and nearly tripled the United Kingdom's electorate.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Representation of the People Act 1918 extended the vote to almost all men over 21 and, for the first time, to most women over 30 in the United Kingdom.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/nineteenth-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nineteenth-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1920</span><span class="path-step-name">Nineteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution</span><span class="path-step-why">Ratified in August 1920, the amendment barred denying citizens the vote on account of sex, though many women of colour remained excluded from voting in practice.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Nineteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution, ratified in August 1920, prohibits the federal government and the states from denying citizens the right to vote on account of sex.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/government/direct-democracy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 508 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Direct democracy</span><span class="path-step-why">Swiss citizens decide by referendum and in open-air assemblies, and only in 1990 did the Federal Supreme Court order Appenzell Innerrhoden to admit women to its Landsgemeinde.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Direct democracy is government by the citizens themselves rather than by their representatives.</span></span></a></li></ol>
